.TH CJSON_DICTIONARY_FIRST 3 "2026-09-29" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_dictionary_first \- start an iteration over the members of an object node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

typedef struct cjson_dictionary_iter {
    const char *key;
    cjson       value;
    /* private fields, whose names begin with an underscore */
} cjson_dictionary_iter;

bool cjson_dictionary_first(cjson dict, cjson_dictionary_iter *it);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Fills the cursor it and steps it onto the first member of the object
node dict; cjson_dictionary_next(3) then steps it onto each later
member.  After a call that returns true, it->key is the key of the
member and it->value is its value, a borrowed reference with the
rules of cjson_dictionary_get(3).

The members come in insertion order, the order in which each key
first entered the object.  For a parsed document that is the order of
the members in the text; for an object that the program builds, it
is the order of the cjson_dictionary_set(3) and cjson_set(3) calls
that added each key.  Replacing the value of a key keeps its place,
while a key that the program removes and then sets again goes after
every other member.  cjson_serialize(3), cjson_serialize_pretty(3)
and cjson_clone(3) use the same order.

The cursor is a plain struct that the caller owns, usually on its own
stack.  The iteration allocates nothing and cannot fail, so there is
nothing to release and the caller can stop at any point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dict  The object node to walk.
it    The cursor to fill.  It must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
true when it now stands on the first member.

false when dict has no members, when dict is NULL, or when dict is
not a CJSON_DICTIONARY; the function then sets it->key and it->value
to NULL.  false also when it is NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
it->key points at the copy of the key that the object stores, and it
stays valid until that member is removed or the object is destroyed.

The cursor holds the member that the next step reads, which is the
successor of the member that the last successful call gave.  It
stays valid across every change to the object that leaves that
successor in place:

  - the replacement of the value of any member, the current one
    included, with cjson_dictionary_set(3) or cjson_set(3).  The
    member keeps its place, so the walk goes on in the same order.
  - the removal of the current member, or of any member other than
    the successor.  To remove the current member, pass it->key to
    cjson_dictionary_remove(3).
  - the insert of a new key.  The new member goes after every other
    member, and whether this walk reaches it is unspecified.

The removal of the successor, with cjson_dictionary_remove(3),
cjson_delete(3) or any other call, invalidates the cursor, and so
does the destroy of the object.  A later cjson_dictionary_next(3) on
an invalid cursor is undefined behavior, so start again with
cjson_dictionary_first() after such a change.

it->key and it->value describe the current member as it was when
the cursor stepped onto it.  cjson_dictionary_set(3) on the key of
the current member destroys the old value, so it->value then names
freed memory; read the new value with cjson_dictionary_get(3) and
it->key.  cjson_set(3), by contrast, updates the existing node in
place, so it->value stays the same node.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
A cjson tree is not thread safe, but several threads can walk the
same object at the same time, each with its own cursor, while no
thread changes the object.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson doc = cjson_parse("{\\"b\\":1,\\"a\\":2}", NULL);
cjson_dictionary_iter it;
for (bool ok = cjson_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cjson_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    printf("%s = %lld\\n", it.key, cjson_int_val(it.value));
}
/* prints "b = 1" and then "a = 2" */

/* Remove every member whose value is null, in one pass. */
for (bool ok = cjson_dictionary_first(doc, &it); ok;
     ok = cjson_dictionary_next(&it)) {
    if (cjson_type(it.value) == CJSON_NULL)
        cjson_dictionary_remove(doc, it.key);
}
cjson_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_dictionary_next (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_size (3),
.BR cjson_serialize (3)
